        <div id="reviews" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Do Buyers Say About Pro JYM Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly praise Pro JYM's taste, especially the chocolate flavors, and mostly describe it as thick and creamy rather than thin. We have not tasted it ourselves; this summary comes from published reviews and retailer feedback. Chocolate Mousse is the flavor reviewers praise most often, and JYM itself highlights Chocolate Cookie Crunch and S'mores as award-winning flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture is the most common talking point. The casein-heavy blend and coconut creamer make a thicker shake than plain whey, which many buyers like because it feels more like a milkshake. Others report that it needs a proper shaker or blender to get rid of small clumps, and that a spoon and glass is not enough. Using the full 6 to 12 oz of liquid JYM recommends, and adding the liquid before the powder, helps.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common complaints fall into three groups. Some reviewers note an artificial sweetener aftertaste in certain flavors, which is not surprising with sucralose and acesulfame potassium on the label. Some describe the vanilla as mild or bland, which suits people who mix it into oats or smoothies but disappoints people drinking it with water. And many buyers say Pro JYM is expensive, though a good share add that they think it is worth the price.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are sensitive to dairy, the casein and milk protein isolate fractions mean Pro JYM contains more lactose than a pure isolate, though JYM does not publish a lactose figure. Buyers who get bloated on regular whey may do better with JYM's Iso JYM, which the brand describes as lactose free, or with an isolate from our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> list. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide explains the difference.
          </p>
