          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pro JYM does what its label says: 24 g of protein per scoop from a disclosed split of whey isolate, micellar casein and milk protein isolate, no amino spiking, and macros of 140 to 160 calories in the core flavors. By our estimate it delivers more casein than whey, which makes it a sensible all-day and bedtime protein and a thicker, more filling shake than plain whey.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The weak points are testing and price. JYM claims no NSF or Informed Sport certification, publishes no batch results, and the label carries a Prop 65 lead warning. At about $1.74 per 25 g in the big Amazon tub it is fairly priced for a blend, but if you only want a post-workout shake, a cheaper whey from our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> list is the better buy.
